DESTROY these habits to ascend your health

Wearing shoes.
When I don’t need them, I don’t wear them. Barefoot walking on safe natural surfaces strengthens the feet, improves sensory feedback from the ground and lets the foot move the way it was designed to.

Training 7 days a week.
More isn’t always better. Training is the stimulus. Food, sleep and recovery are what allow adaptation to actually happen. Four focused sessions works far better than seven mediocre ones.

Being afraid of having “too much milk.”
Milk is one of the most nutrient-dense drinks available. High-quality protein, calcium, phosphorus, potassium, carbohydrates and fats, plus excellent hydration. Just watch the calories and get a good source (I go raw and local)

Working inside when I don’t need to.
If a call can be taken outside, I take it outside. More steps, natural daylight, fresh air and less unnecessary time staring at artificial light and screens.

Sitting down after big meals.
walk. Even 10 minutes of movement after eating can improve post-meal blood glucose control, digestion, and sleep, while adding effortless movement to my day.

Long cardio sessions.
Excessive long distance endurance work just becomes another source of physiological stress. walk for daily movement, sprint for HIIT. There’s a reason world class long distance runners look so unhealthy compared to world class sprinters. (They are)

Stressing over things I can’t control.
Chronic stress has physical consequences. It is your responsibility to do the mental work to cultivate a low stress mental state.

Neglecting orofacial training.
Your tongue, jaw and suprahyoid muscles are functional muscles. They contribute to swallowing and airway mechanics, and not training them is costing you your airway health and attractiveness.

Lying in.
get up and move. Movement supports normal lymphatic circulation, and getting outside into morning daylight gives your circadian clock a strong signal that the day has started.

Mouth breathing.
You are recessing your upper jaw if your tongue is not in contact with your palatine suture. (On the roof of your mouth)
Nasal breathing is superior.
